course: dictionary
lesson: 14804
title: Words about buying
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Purchasable is an adjective. It describes something that can be bought, or is for sale. Purchase is a formal word for buy, and the ending -able means "can be done", so purchasable means "can be bought".
explain: You will meet it on notices, in games and in shops: «Extra lives are purchasable in the app.» This tells you the game may ask you to pay for extra lives.
explain: In a maths problem, purchasable tells you what you can buy with the money you have. When you read a sentence with purchasable in it, try replacing it with "can be bought" or "for sale". If the sentence still makes sense, you have understood it.
fact: Purchasable means able to be bought, or available for sale.
fact: Purchase is a formal word for buy. The ending -able means "can be".
fact: «Tickets are purchasable online» means you can buy the tickets on the internet.
fact: In everyday speech, people say "you can buy it" or "it is for sale".
fact: «Extra lives are purchasable in the app» is a warning that a game may ask you for money.
exercise: «The tickets for the show are purchasable online.» What does purchasable mean? | Able to be lost | *Able to be bought | Able to be broken | Able to be seen from far away
exercise: A game shows «Extra lives are purchasable in the app.» What does this warn Mia? | The game is only for babies | *The game may ask her to pay for extra lives | The game is free and will give her extra lives for nothing | Her extra lives will disappear
exercise: «Sam has £10. Which item is purchasable with his money?» | *A pencil that costs 50p | A house that costs £200,000 | A bike that costs £150 | A car that costs £9000
test: «The sweets on the top shelf are purchasable at the counter.» What does purchasable mean here? | *Able to be bought | Able to be counted | Able to be eaten for free | Able to be stacked on a shelf
test: Which everyday words mean the same as purchasable? | Broken and lost | Quiet and slow | Happy and free | *For sale and you can buy it
exam: «The toys in the window are purchasable with birthday money.» Which words in the sentence tell you the toys can be bought? | toys in the window | are | the | *purchasable with birthday money
